<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uwajima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uwajima is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehime-prefecture">Ehime Prefecture</a> on the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, facing the Bungo Channel. Its castle, rebuilt by the Date family in the 1660s, is one of the few in Japan with an original keep. From 1614 to 1871 it was the seat of the Uwajima domain, ruled by a branch of the Date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a>, and it is known for pearl farming and bull-sumo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uwajima.jpg" alt="Uwajima" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reggaeman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uwajima_Bay.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uwajima lies in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehime-prefecture">Ehime Prefecture</a>, facing a wide rias coastline and remote islands on Uwa Bay, with mountains on its other three sides. It covers 468.19 square kilometres and has a humid subtropical climate with hot summers and cool winters, and its heaviest rain falls from June to September. It neighbours Ainan, Kihoku, Matsuno and Seiyo in Ehime and Shimanto and Sukumo in Kōchi Prefecture. Many people think of it as the westernmost city in Ehime, although Yawatahama lies to its north-northwest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area belonged to ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyo-province">Iyo Province</a>. In the Heian period Uwajima Bay, notably the island of Hiburijima, was a centre of piracy in the Seto Inland Sea and the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-sumitomo">Fujiwara no Sumitomo</a> in his rebellion. In the Muromachi period the Ashikaga shogunate appointed a branch of the Saionji family as governor. It was repeatedly attacked by stronger neighbours, among them Ouchi Yoshitaka, Mōri Motonari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-sorin">Ōtomo Sōrin</a> and the Chōsokabe, and was overcome by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chōsokabe Motochika</a>, who was in turn overthrown by the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>.</p><p>After Hideyoshi's settlement of Iyo, Toda Katsutaka held the area. He ruled with extreme harshness, and when ordered to send troops to the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1592 he cut down large trees in shrines and temples to build ships. He died in Korea without an heir. Hideyoshi then gave the domain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todo-takatora">Tōdō Takatora</a>, a noted castle designer who spent six years building Itajima Castle, later called Uwajima Castle. In 1614 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-hidemune">Date Hidemune</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> by a concubine, was awarded the 100,000 koku Uwajima Domain by the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a>, and moved into the castle the following year. His descendants ruled the domain until the Meiji restoration.</p><p>The modern town of Uwajima was created after the restoration and was raised to city status on 1 August 1921 after merging with the village of Yahata. Bombing in 1945 destroyed much of the centre, but reconstruction was rapid. Neighbouring villages and, on 1 August 2005, the towns of Mima, Tsushima and Yoshida were merged into the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Uwajima is the largest municipality of southwestern Ehime. Its economy rests on commercial fishing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquaculture">aquaculture</a> and cultured pearls, and it has a single shipyard. Uwajima Castle is one of the 12 Japanese castles with an original donjon from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The Date Family Museum holds objects tied to the region and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> family, and Ryūkō-ji and Butsumoku-ji are the 41st and 42nd temples on the Shikoku Pilgrimage. The Ushi-oni Festival, held in July, includes a parade of ushioni teams, dance and a bullfight, in which two bulls fight in a ring until one flees or its knees touch the ground.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uwajima, Ehime” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1614 the shogunate granted <b>Uwajima</b> in Iyo province, with 100,000 koku, to Date Hidemune, the eldest son of Date Masamune, who had been passed over as heir to the Sendai Domain.</blockquote><a href="/state/uwajima-domain">Uwajima Domain</a></li></ul></section>
